 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "<table class=\"unnumbered full-width\">\n<thead>\n<tr><td valign=\"top\" align=\"center\"><strong>Dataset D Description</strong></td>\n<td valign=\"top\" align=\"center\"><strong>Data</strong></td>\n\n</tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td valign=\"top\" align=\"left\">Credit card balances for consumers (in dollars)</td>\n<td valign=\"top\" align=\"left\">Sample of 9 consumers: $878.32, $671.54, $990.35, $801.85, $4303.76, $1285.03, $289.44, $78.61, $952.83</td>\n\n</tr></tbody>\n\n</table>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 461521,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "For Dataset D, calculate the quartiles of the dataset.\n\n\n\n",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "To find the first quartile using Excel, copy the dataset into cells A1 to A11. In any empty cell, type in: <br>\n=QUARTILE.EXC(A1:A11,1)<br><br>\nTo find the second quartile using Excel, copy the dataset into cells A1 to A11. In any empty cell, type in: <br>\n=QUARTILE.EXC(A1:A11,2)<br><br>\nTo find the third quartile using Excel, copy the dataset into cells A1 to A11. In any empty cell, type in:<br>\n=QUARTILE.EXC(A1:A11,3)<br><br>\nTo find the quartiles using Python,\n<pre class=\"python\" lang=\"python\"># import the statistics library\nimport statistics\n# define a dataset of credit card balances\nbalances = [878.32, 671.54, 990.35, 801.85, 4303.76, 1285.03, 289.44, 78.61, 952.83, 386.45, 1182.42]\n# execute the statistics.quantiles() function on this list, using n = 4 to specify quartiles.\nprint('quartiles = ',statistics.quantiles(balances, n = 4))\n</pre>\n\n\n"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"{Q_1} = 386.45,\\;{Q_2} = 878.32,\"></span> <span data-math=\"{Q_3} = 1182.42\"></span>\n\n"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 461522,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "For this same dataset, calculate the interquartile range (IQR).",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"\\mathrm{IQR}=795.97\"></span>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "The interquartile range is calculated as the third quartile, subtract the first quartile, as follows:<br>\n<span data-math=\"IQR = {Q_3} - {Q_1} = 1182.42 - 386.45 = 795.97\"></span>"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 461523,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Determine if there are any potential outliers in this dataset.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"\\mathrm{Outlier}\\;\\mathrm{data}\\;\\mathrm{value} = 4303.76\"></span>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "To determine if there are outliers in the dataset, first calculate the lower and upper bounds for outliers, use the following formulas:<br>\n<span data-math=\"\\text{Lower Bound for Outliers}=Q_1-1.5\\cdot\\mathrm{IQR}=386.45\\;–\\;(1.5\\cdot795.97)=-807.505\"></span><br>\n<span data-math=\"\\text{Upper Bound for Outliers}=Q_3+1.5\\cdot\\mathrm{IQR}=1182.42+(1.5\\cdot795.97)=2376.375\"></span><br>There are no data values below the lower bound.<br>However, there is one data value above the upper bound of 2376.375, namely the data value 4303.76. Thus, there is one outlier data value of 4303.76.\n"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
